  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\n\nBrief Synopsis...\n\nHigher pressure across the southeast U.S. with a cutoff low centered \nacross northern California and southwest Oregon today, keeps the \nCentral Plains region underneath southwest flow. This pattern tends \nto be fairly unstable as it only takes a weak embedded shortwave \ndisturbance to initiate scattered showers or thunderstorms (lower \nthan usual short-term forecast confidence). The better forcing for \nwidespread precipitation will come over the weekend when the cutoff \nlow weakens back to a broad trough and takes a run at the central \nU.S. Overall, high temperatures will be on track to peak for the \nweek on Friday (mid 80s to low 90s) before embracing the 70s for \nmuch of the start of next week. \n\n\nTonight...\n\nLow-level stratus continues to loom across the area this afternoon, \nhelping to keep highs from escaping the 70s. Though a few pockets of \ndrizzle or light rain can't be ruled out tonight (20-50% chance), \nthe latest models have seemed to back off some with the \nprecipitation potential (10-30% HREF probs). In all, the best time \nwindow for any coverage of scattered light rain showers will mainly \noccur between the 1-9AM time range. Accumulations will be minimal \n(<0.25\") with most areas only receiving a trace to a few thousandths \nof an inch of precip. The other main weather concern tonight would \nbe for the possibility of dense fog. Virtually all HREF members \n(80%) including the RRFS, point towards a widespread coverage of \ndense fog tonight. Given the weak easterly surface winds (upslope \nflow) with high moisture concentrations (60s dewpoints), the \npotential for fog development appears almost certain. In addition, \nforecast lows are projected to reach the 60s (approaching dewpoints), \na high indicator for condensation or fog. \n\n\nThursday & Beyond...\n\nTemperatures will gradually rise a through Friday (mid 80s to low \n90s highs) as clearing skies couple with southerly warm air \nadvecting winds once more. This heat will likely remain short-lived \nas a cold frontal passage overnight Friday flips the winds north \nagain, dragging in a cooler and drier airmass. Highs on the \nother side look to return to the 70s to mid 80s Saturday and the \nupper 60s and 70s Sunday through Tuesday.  \n\nThe next best chance for precipitation (20-50%) lies overnight \nFriday with the coming of the cold front (and the upper-level \ntrough/disturbance aloft). The main uncertainty at the moment would be \nfor how far south and west thunderstorms are able to form. The \nlatest high-resolution models/CAMs paint much of northeast Nebraska \nto be under siege from these storms with a few initializing across \ncentral nebraska. Conditions wise, the instability axis (the most \ncommon path for storms to follow ... where CAPE >2,000J/kg) is \nmodeled to set up just northeast of the Tri-Cities. The factors \nthat, however, keep confidence for severe convection low for now \nwould be from minimal shear (20-30kts) and degrading lapse rates \novernight that could thwart overall storm intensity. In case of an \nearlier arrival of storms Friday evening instead of night (slightly \nmore severe conducing ingredients), the SPC includes the southeast \n2/3rd of the area under a marginal risk (level 1 of 5) for severe \nweather. Scattered storms chance return Saturday and Sunday. \n\nLooking well beyond for the end of week forecast next week, highs may \nbe possible to warm back up a few degrees. Cluster analysis begins to \ndiverge between the next Wednesday to next Saturday timeframe as \nthe ensembles start to show more variance (spread of possibilities). \nThe leading cluster, explaining around 40% of the variance, favors \nbroad ridging in the central U.S. This pattern would support the \nwarmer temperatures and possibly a less frequency of precipitation \nchances.",
